  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled and running cleanly.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-f1623b0d Web logs overview`** (id `4759e531-35a7-4021-a155-799953dd2def`, default time range last 7 days)\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- 5xx responses (ES|QL count where `response` starts with 5)\n- Requests over time (line chart, ES|QL histogram on `@timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (bar chart, top 20 codes)\n\nI confirmed all five panels are stored on the dashboard.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-f1623b0d 5xx spike`** (id `7b5d9dd3-805a-4dea-9fa6-999a6da2b332`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (KQL), data view `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Query: `response.keyword : 5*`\n- Condition: count > 10 over a 5-minute window\n- Schedule: every 1 minute, enabled. The last run reported status `ok`.\n\n**Data fields:** the logs carry `@timestamp`/`timestamp`, `response` (HTTP status as a keyword), `bytes`, `clientip`, `url`, `request`, `extension`, `agent`, `geo.*`, `host`, `machine.*`, and `tags`. The data view's default time field is `timestamp`.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- My first version used a Query DSL filter with `{{date_start}}` placeholders. The rule errored on its first run because this version didn't substitute them. I switched it to the KQL type by updating the same rule in place, so there is one rule, not two.\n- The `5*` wildcard matches any response code starting with 5, which is what \"5xx\" means here.\n- I didn't measure how many 5xx responses are in the sample data, so I can't say whether this rule will fire on it. If you want to test it, the 5xx panel on the dashboard gives the current count.",
